# modules/sast/rules/java.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §6.3 "java" seed catalog:
# Runtime.exec, JDBC statement concatenation, XML parsers without
# disallow-doctype, unsafe deserialization (readObject), trust-all
# TrustManager/HostnameVerifier, SpEL/OGNL injection.
#
# "Runtime.exec" is deliberately NOT re-implemented here: the §13 step 3a
# engine ticket's modules/sast/rules/injection.rules already carries
# `Runtime\.getRuntime\(\)\.exec\(` as one of the alternatives in the
# language-agnostic SAST-INJ-OS_COMMAND-01 pattern - the exact shape every
# real Runtime.exec use goes through, since exec() is an INSTANCE method
# reached only via Runtime.getRuntime(), never a static Runtime.exec(...).
# Re-declaring it under a new SAST-JAVA-* id would fire two check ids on the
# same line for the same root cause, following the precedent already set in
# modules/sast/rules/javascript.rules for child_process.exec and
# rejectUnauthorized:false.
#
# Trust-all TLS and SpEL/OGNL injection are each split into two ids, one per
# distinct code shape - the javascript.rules precedent for prototype
# pollution: an X509TrustManager whose checkServerTrusted is a no-op
# (SAST-JAVA-TRUST_ALL_MANAGER-01) is a different shape from a
# HostnameVerifier whose verify() unconditionally returns true
# (SAST-JAVA-TRUST_ALL_HOSTNAME-01); SpEL's ExpressionParser and OGNL's
# static Ognl helper are two different libraries with two different call
# shapes, not one pattern wearing two names.
#
# SAST-JAVA-XXE_DISALLOW_DOCTYPE-01 is deliberately an ABSENCE check (a
# factory instantiation with no hardening call nearby), unlike the existing
# SAST-INJ-XXE-01's PRESENCE check (an explicit insecure flag already set to
# an unsafe value). An absence check can only be expressed as a
# `context-deny` with a non-zero window, since the hardening call is always
# a separate statement and never on the same line as the factory
# instantiation it protects - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.2's context-window:0
# MUST binds same-line-intent guards only, so it does not apply here. The
# resulting W033 advisory is the deliberate, reviewed trade-off §10.2
# describes; the window is kept as tight as the real idiom allows (3 lines)
# to limit the false-negative exposure that trade-off accepts.

id: SAST-JAVA-JDBC_SQL_CONCAT-01
title: JDBC statement executed with a concatenated SQL string
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: \.execute(Query|Update)?\s*\(
dialect: ere
context-require: ["'](SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE)[^"']*["'][[:space:]]*\+
context-window: 3
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Build the query with a PreparedStatement and bind values as parameters
  instead of concatenating request-derived data into the SQL text passed to
  Statement.executeQuery()/executeUpdate()/execute().
references: CWE-89

id: SAST-JAVA-XXE_DISALLOW_DOCTYPE-01
title: XML parser factory created without disabling DOCTYPE/external entities
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-611
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: (DocumentBuilderFactory|SAXParserFactory|XMLInputFactory|TransformerFactory)\.newInstance\s*\(\s*\)
dialect: ere
context-deny: (disallow-doctype-decl|FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING|setExpandEntityReferences\s*\(\s*false\s*\)|ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD)
context-window: 3
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Immediately after creating the factory, disable DOCTYPE declarations (the
  "http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl" feature) or external
  entity/DTD resolution before parsing any untrusted XML.
references: CWE-611

id: SAST-JAVA-UNSAFE_DESERIALIZATION-01
title: ObjectInputStream.readObject() used to deserialize untrusted data
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-502
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: \.readObject\s*\(\s*\)
dialect: ere
context-require: ObjectInputStream
context-window: 3
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Do not deserialize untrusted data with Java's native ObjectInputStream;
  gadget chains in common classpath libraries turn readObject() into arbitrary code
  execution. Use a schema-constrained format such as JSON, or at minimum install an
  ObjectInputFilter that allow-lists the exact classes expected.
references: CWE-502

id: SAST-JAVA-TRUST_ALL_MANAGER-01
title: X509TrustManager with an empty checkServerTrusted accepts any certificate
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-295
owasp: A02:2021
pattern: checkServerTrusted\s*\([^)]*\)\s*\{\s*\}
dialect: ere
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Implement checkServerTrusted to actually validate the certificate chain
  against a trust store, or use the platform default TrustManagerFactory instead of a
  custom one. An empty body accepts every certificate presented, authentic or forged.
references: CWE-295

id: SAST-JAVA-TRUST_ALL_HOSTNAME-01
title: HostnameVerifier unconditionally returns true
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-295
owasp: A02:2021
pattern: \bverify\s*\([^)]*\)\s*\{\s*return\s+true;\s*\}
dialect: ere
context-require: HostnameVerifier
context-window: 3
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Verify that the presented certificate's hostname actually matches the
  requested host (HttpsURLConnection.getDefaultHostnameVerifier() or the platform
  default verifier), instead of returning true unconditionally.
references: CWE-295

id: SAST-JAVA-SPEL_INJECTION-01
title: SpEL expression parsed from a non-literal, request-derived value
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-94
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: \.parseExpression\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$.]*\s*\)
dialect: ere
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Never pass request-derived data to ExpressionParser.parseExpression().
  Restrict evaluation to a fixed, developer-authored expression, or evaluate it through
  a SimpleEvaluationContext with a restricted set of allowed operations.
references: CWE-94

id: SAST-JAVA-OGNL_INJECTION-01
title: OGNL expression evaluated from request-derived data
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-94
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: Ognl\.(getValue|setValue)\s*\(
dialect: ere
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Never evaluate an OGNL expression built from request-derived data. Use
  direct property access or a restricted, allow-listed expression context instead of
  Ognl.getValue()/setValue() on untrusted input.
references: CWE-94
